WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.960
- So today Sinead and I are gonna speak

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:05.843
about how math are used to
model the epidemic of COVID-19.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:07.960
But we're just gonna start

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.893
with a little bit of
background about ourselves.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.080
So I'm Sinead, I'm originally
from Glasgow in Scotland.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.320
And now I'm working at Columbia University

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.220
in New York as a postdoc.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.330
And like Jeanne said, my research focuses

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.820
on using mathematical models to understand

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.043
how our immune systems
respond to virus infections.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.870
- And I'm Chloé, and I'm
originally from Toulouse

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.220
in the southwest of France.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.680
And I moved to New York early last year.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.040
I'm also a postdoc in Columbia

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.510
in the same team as Sinead and
working on mathematical models

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.960
of the immune system as well.

00:00:44.997 --> 00:00:47.200
- So just some information about me.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.550
I've been working in New
York for two years now.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.070
And usually I love to travel

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.860
so this is me and my boyfriend

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.900
on a trip to Scotland last summer.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.370
But since the outbreak started,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.260
we've been sheltering at
home with our dog Dixie,

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.093
and you can probably tell

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:02.520
that she's not really a fan
of posing for photographs

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.460
by her face here.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.820
But she is very happy

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.380
that we're both working from home now

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.820
and can keep her company all day.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:09.810
And in my spare time,

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.220
I've started taking an
online drawing class

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.613
just to improve my art
skills a little bit.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.610
- And I moved to New York
City early last year,

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.310
I've been really enjoying
exploring the city,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.940
you can see a picture
of my good friend Kalina

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.300
and I in Greenpoint,

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.100
where you have a pretty great
view of the skyline.

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:32.820
And since the beginning of the epidemic,

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.640
I've been staying with my boyfriend,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.690
working from his place.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.820
I'm getting to know his cat Lily,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.360
and you can see it's doing well

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.790
'cause she's taking selfie with me now.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:44.770
And I'm also cooking a lot

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.890
and I'm trying to find the
best cookies recipe ever.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.990
And this is one of my
experiment from a few weeks ago

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:52.090
that you can see here.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:56.860
- So before we get started,

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:57.860
we just want to let you know

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.040
that our presentation will
be a little bit different

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.060
from previous D4Ps,

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.460
because most of the
papers presented before,

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.690
used reasoning that was
based on experimental data,

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.300
but we're gonna present another style

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.390
of scientific reasoning
called mathematical

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.510
and hypothetical modeling.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.100
And essentially what that means is that,

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.890
if you want to understand
a particular process,

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.480
you can use mathematical
models to simplify

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:21.880
and represent it.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.230
And today, we're gonna show
how those simplified models

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:27.063
can be used to understand
the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:30.750
And so you may have noticed

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.600
that this style of scientific reasoning

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.110
has got a lot of media attention recently,

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:38.120
as governments and policymakers
have been consulting

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.700
with scientists and using
their mathematical models

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.720
to help plan pandemic response efforts.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.760
So you may be familiar with terms like,

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.080
flattening the curve,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.780
modeling the impacts of social distancing,

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.410
and the value of a
quantity called R0.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.290
And we'll be discussing
all of these things today

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.640
as we go through the paper.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.143
So here is the paper.

00:02:59.143 --> 00:03:01.720
It's called "The effect
of control strategies

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.910
to reduce social mixing on outcomes

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:06.910
of the COVID-19 epidemic in Wuhan, China:

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:08.830
a modeling study."

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:10.610
So it's quite a complicated title.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.030
But if you focus on the phrase,

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.040
control strategies to
reduce social mixing,

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.270
that essentially means the
rules of social distancing.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.300
And so what the authors do,

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.360
is they use mathematical modeling

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.740
to explore what would happen
to the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.640
when you lift the rules
of social distancing.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.400
And the use of Wuhan in China

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.700
where the virus first
emerged as a case study.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.840
And so you might be wondering, why Wuhan?

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.590
And an important point to know is

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.920
that this paper was published in March.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.180
And at that time, Wuhan
was still very much

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.320
a global focus of the outbreak

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:45.610
and social distancing rules

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:47.830
had already been established there.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:50.920
And so learning what might
happen when you lift those rules,

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.620
could help us understand
how epidemics will play out

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.163
in other locations as well.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:58.303
And another thing we want to
mention about this paper is

00:03:58.303 --> 00:04:02.200
that it's already been
cited over 170 times,

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.710
which means that over 170 other papers

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.820
that have been written just since March,

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.060
have either mentioned or used the results

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.810
from this paper in some way.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.840
And that's a huge number
in just two months.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.610
And it really shows how fast the pace

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.687
of research is moving in
response to the pandemic.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:21.380
Another cool thing we want to highlight

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.930
is that all of the authors are
based in London, in the UK.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.220
They have experienced
modeling infectious diseases

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:29.640
around the world.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.990
And so some of the other
sightings that they've worked on,

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.970
are Ebola in West Africa,
Dengue in Indonesia,

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:40.120
Zika in Singapore and
Tuberculosis in Cambodia.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.840
And so this just goes to show that,

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:43.360
mathematical modeling isn't just used

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.560
in the fight against SARS-CoV-2

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.620
but in the fight against
infectious diseases

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:48.937
around the world.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:53.050
- Sinead just showed you

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:54.850
that this learning team has been working

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.980
on modeling a different
number of infectious disease,

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.660
and actually one of the first question

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.590
you could ask is, this
why do we use model?

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.110
Which is a legitimate question to ask.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.920
So basically, when a disease
spreads in the population,

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.960
a lot of questions are raised,

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:10.980
and in particular,

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.710
how fast and why disease spreads.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.220
And those questions are really important.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.230
And eventually by
knowing a little bit more

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.560
about the disease, we can also determine

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.550
how to slow down the
spread of the disease.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.370
And basically mathematical models

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:29.110
can be used to capture the
standard transmission process

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:31.440
and study which interventions can be used

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.300
and can be helpful.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.320
And actually studying
infectious disease is not new.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.030
And for example, one of the pioneers

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.730
in this field was John Snow.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:44.510
So some of you may actually
be familiar with his name.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.620
He's a famous character of
the Game of Thrones series,

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.260
but the real John Snow was
actually an English physician

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:51.673
in the 19th century.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.050
And here is the real John Snow

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:59.310
and he's known for his
work on a cholera outbreak

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:02.620
that was going on in London in 1854.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:03.510
And at the moment,

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:06.230
people didn't know where
the disease was coming from.

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.160
There was no knowledge about germs

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.650
and microbes as we know now.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:11.920
And it was basically thought

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.653
that the disease was caused
by the bad quality of air.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:19.100
But John Snow at the moment,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.960
studied the pattern of infections
in London neighborhoods

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.960
and he understood, by
studying those patterns,

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:29.230
that cholera was transmitted by something,

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:33.320
by an agent in contaminated water.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.070
And so that led the
government to take actions,

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:37.980
including replacing the pumps

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:39.840
and that overall improved the quality

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.633
of water people had access to.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.360
And I wanna just quickly
mention some other pioneers

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.320
who studied infectious disease.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.460
So here you've got Anna Wessels Williams,

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.490
and she was actually a New Yorker

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.870
and she was one of the
frontline scientists

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:02.170
working on measures of fighting
the pandemic of flu in 1918.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:03.700
And you may have heard about this pandemic

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:07.700
'cause people have been
speaking a lot about it lately.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:09.800
And here is Fe del Mundo

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.620
and she worked in the 50's,

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:14.270
on understanding the transmission

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:17.450
and the characteristics of
Dengue in the Philippines.

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:19.210
Dengue being a tropical disease

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:21.113
that's transmitted by mosquitoes.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.370
- Okay, so now let's
get back to the paper.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:27.420
And today we're gonna focus

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.760
on two of the author's main objectives.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.220
So objective one, was to
use a model to predict

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:33.610
what would happen in Wuhan

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.610
if and when social
distancing rules are lifted.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.380
And then objective two, was to explore

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:40.850
how transmission from children
affects the progression

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:42.420
of the epidemic.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.150
And the reason that they
included this second objective,

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.090
is that when the work was carried out,

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:48.490
observations had been made

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.300
that children seemed to be less affected

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.830
by the disease than adults.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:52.840
But it wasn't really clear

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.600
whether children were
transmitting the disease

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.760
as much as adults, which
is still the case now.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.570
And so the authors wanted to see

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:00.320
how that could impact the outbreak.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:04.030
But before we jump into the paper,

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:06.500
there are a few key terms
that we want to go through

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:08.370
that may be unfamiliar,

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:10.700
but they're used a lot in
the field of epidemiology.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:11.730
And they'll become important

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.240
when we get into the details of the paper.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.360
And so some of the things
we'll go through are the latent

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.253
and infectious periods, R0 and Re,

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:21.567
and then also what the
transmission rate is

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:23.063
and what a simulation is.

00:08:25.877 --> 00:08:28.410
And so first, the latent
and infectious periods.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:29.410
So if you think about someone

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:31.840
who's just been infected with the virus,

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.400
it takes some time for the
virus to enter their cells

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:35.970
and to grow in numbers.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:39.080
And during that time period,
although they're infected,

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.287
they're not yet infectious.

00:08:40.287 --> 00:08:43.570
And so they're not yet capable
of infecting someone else.

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:45.970
And we call this time
period, the latent period.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:49.850
Then after some time,
they'll become infectious

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:50.683
and at that point,

00:08:50.683 --> 00:08:53.530
they will be able to infect
or transmit the virus

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:54.940
to someone else.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:55.773
And so then we see

00:08:55.773 --> 00:08:57.480
that they've entered the infectious period

00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:59.280
and they're capable of transmission.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.410
And then finally, after
some point in time,

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:04.220
they'll recover from the disease,

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.300
or in very unfortunate circumstances,

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.120
will die from the disease.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.640
And at that point, because
they're no longer infected,

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.370
and all transmission has stopped,

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.870
we see that they've entered
the recovered period.

00:09:18.188 --> 00:09:19.021
And so another important term

00:09:19.021 --> 00:09:20.140
that we want to go over,

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:23.570
is R0 or the basic reproduction number.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:24.713
And its full definition is,

00:09:24.713 --> 00:09:27.450
that it's the average number of new cases,

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.520
caused by each infectious person

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:30.970
at the start of the epidemic.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.800
And it essentially measures the potential

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:35.923
for a disease to spread in a population.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.540
So if you imagine that you're
the first person infected

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.650
with a new disease, then R0
is the number of new people

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.380
that you're going to infect,

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:45.650
and also the number of new people

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:48.100
that those people will go
on to infect and so on.

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:52.480
And so in this slide we showed
an example of R0 equal to two.

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.640
So you start with one infected person,

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.470
and then that infected person goes on

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.370
to infect two new people.

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:00.240
And then each of those two people goes on

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.010
to infect two more new people,

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.120
which leads to four people infected,

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.423
and so on, and so on.

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:08.460
And so in reality, you may find

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.600
that some people only affect one person

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.920
and maybe others infect three people.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:15.300
But on average, we see that it's two.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.560
And from the picture you
can see here very quickly,

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.890
we can build up to having a lot

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:20.340
of new people being infected.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:23.930
And so one important question is,

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:27.030
how can we use R0 at
the start of an outbreak

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.990
to predict whether the disease
will successfully spread

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:31.170
in the population?

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.170
And actually, it's pretty simple,

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.680
because if each infectious person infects

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.280
less than one new person on average,

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.360
then the number of new cases will decrease

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.140
and the disease will fade out.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:45.460
And so in other words,
if R0 is less than one,

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:46.710
the disease won't spread.

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:50.270
But if R0 is greater than
one, like in the example,

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:52.620
then the number of new
cases will continue to grow,

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:54.170
and so the disease will spread.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.260
And this is just to give
you an example of how the R0

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:03.860
for SARS-CoV-2 compares to
other infectious diseases.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:05.020
And so the value for this,

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:06.600
because we're looking at today,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:07.973
is the use value of 2.5.

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.555
And you may have seen some
other values being used,

00:11:11.555 --> 00:11:13.380
and that's because there
is some uncertainty

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.410
around the true number.

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:17.530
And this is partly due to the fact

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:20.810
that people use different
data to estimate these things.

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.310
But if we think about the value of 2.5,

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:24.870
we can compare that to other diseases

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:26.350
and we can see that seasonal flu

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:30.700
and Ebola both have values
that are less than 2.5,

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.850
whereas smallpox and SARS-CoV-1,

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:36.450
which was the virus that caused
the SARS epidemic in 2003,

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:38.540
they both have moderately higher R0s

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:40.060
between three and five,

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:43.123
whereas measles has a
much, much higher R0 at 15.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:45.710
And it's worth just pointing out here

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:47.560
that R0 doesn't tell you anything

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:49.400
about how severe the disease is,

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:50.923
but just how well it spreads.

00:11:54.629 --> 00:11:56.800
And so one quantity
that's very similar to R0,

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.910
which you may have also
heard about recently,

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:02.300
is the effect of
reproduction number, or Re,

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.480
or sometimes it's also called RT,

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:05.833
and sometimes just R.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:11.190
And like R0, RT measures the
average number of new cases

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:13.400
caused by each infectious person.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.520
But instead of being used at
the start of the outbreak,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.713
we use it once the outbreak has taken off.

00:12:19.779 --> 00:12:22.403
And so if you remember our
example of R0 equals two,

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.370
since it's at the start of the outbreak,

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:27.850
no one has had the disease yet

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:30.430
and so everyone is at
risk of being infected.

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:33.013
But if you compare that
same example for Re,

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:36.260
we can see that now on average,

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:39.170
each person is infecting less new people.

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:42.220
And that's because since the
outbreak has taken off now,

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:45.200
some people have already had
the disease and recovered.

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:46.700
And so at least in the short term,

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:48.570
they can't be infected again.

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:52.260
And so the value of Re, is
less than the value of R0,

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.410
because there are less
people available to infect,

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.163
once the epidemic has taken off.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:12:59.670
And so one thing you might have noticed,

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:03.000
is that R0 is fixed at
the start of an epidemic

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.420
and doesn't change,

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:06.800
whereas Re can change over time,

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:09.490
as the number of people
who've recovered changes.

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:11.850
And so Re is also a very important measure

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:14.610
because we can use it at
any point in the outbreak,

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:18.130
to determine whether the
disease will continue to spread,

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.763
like we did with R0 at
the start at the outbreak.

00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:23.823
And so if Re is less than one,

00:13:23.823 --> 00:13:25.430
then each infectious person,

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.600
will infect less than one
new person on average.

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:31.510
And so the number of
new cases will decrease.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.710
Whereas if Re greater than one,

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.060
then the number of new
cases will continue too.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.143
- I think we have that poll now.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.370
- Okay, so here's our
question for the poll.

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.460
The question is, imagine
you were the first person

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:01.720
infected by SARS-CoV-2 or Ebola,

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:04.780
to how many people would
you transmit the virus?

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:06.130
Is it less than two?

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:07.820
Between two and four?

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:09.230
Between four and six?

00:14:09.230 --> 00:14:10.263
Or more than six?

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:15.490
So we'll give you a few moments
just to put in your answers.

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:39.100
Okay, that's great.

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:41.280
So I think most people have voted now

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:42.700
and it seems like, yup,

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:43.930
most of you got the answer right.

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:45.770
It's between two and four

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:48.357
'cause you saw that the
R0 for SARS-CoV-2 was 2.5

00:14:48.357 --> 00:14:52.323
and for Ebola, it was two, great.

00:14:58.030 --> 00:15:01.640
Okay, so one last quantity
that's also related to R0,

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:03.560
is the transmission rate.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:06.410
And essentially for
each infectious person,

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:08.500
that measures how many people

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.413
that they will infect on each
day that they're infectious.

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:14.650
So for example, if I stay at home

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:17.280
and I don't come into contact with anyone,

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:19.150
and am infectious,

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:20.410
but because I haven't seen anyone,

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:23.610
I won't be able to actually infect anyone.

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.800
But if I decide to go out one day,

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:28.690
and I have contact with five other people,

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:30.000
like in the example,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.150
and if the chance of infecting somebody

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:34.730
who I have contact with is one in five,

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:38.130
then overall, I'll end
up infecting five people.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:38.963
And so you can see

00:15:38.963 --> 00:15:40.380
that the transmission
rate not only depends

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:42.360
on the number of contacts that you have,

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:43.530
but also on the chance

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.930
that each of those contacts
results in transmission

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:46.763
of the disease.

00:15:48.432 --> 00:15:50.930
And so as an example, at
the start of the epidemic,

00:15:50.930 --> 00:15:53.780
we can link the transmission rate to R0,

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:57.440
because if I infect people
or one person each day

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:00.650
that I'm infected, so my
transmission rate is one

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:02.980
and I end up being
infectious for two days.

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:04.777
Then overall, I'll infect two people,

00:16:04.777 --> 00:16:07.933
and so that would correspond
to a value of R0 equal to two.

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:15.660
- So the final term we
wanna go over is simulation.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:18.760
And that term relates to how we use models

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:20.710
to make predictions.

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:22.790
So for example, if I wanna use a model

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:25.773
to look at how a disease will
spread in a city like Wuhan,

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:29.650
I need to know some of
the value of the key terms

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:33.950
like transmission rate or R0
that Sinead just introduced.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:36.970
And really often we don't
know the real value,

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:40.530
but we have an idea of
the range of those values.

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:42.670
So for example, I may
know from other studies,

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:45.383
that R0 is somewhere between two and four.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:49.750
Choosing values in this range,

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:52.730
so for example, here I choose R0 equal two

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:54.400
and then I run the model

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:55.680
and I look at what happens.

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.030
So here for example,

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.720
I look at the number of cases over time

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:02.243
to see how the outbreak progresses.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:06.367
But as Sinead just
said, R0 is a key term..

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:10.310
Is very likely to change the outcome.

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:12.500
So here's the number of cases.

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.610
So what I can do after that,

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:17.880
is taking R0 equal to four instead of two.

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:19.340
And then I run the model again,

00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:23.460
and I see how that modifies the
progression of the outbreak.

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:25.490
And so each time we run the model,

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:28.130
we call that a simulation.

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:30.920
And then R0 is called a parameter

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:32.830
because it's a value we can modify

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:35.040
each time we run the model.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.630
So other important parameters,

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:39.567
are actually the transmission rate,

00:17:39.567 --> 00:17:42.460
the infectious period
and the latent period

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:44.077
that were just explained.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:51.600
Okay, so now that we went
through all of the key terms,

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:52.900
let's go back to the paper.

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:56.050
So that's just a reminder
of the key objectives.

00:17:56.050 --> 00:17:59.190
The first objective happens

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:02.620
when the social distancing
rules are lifted.

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:03.810
And then the second objective,

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.720
is to explore how the transmission

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:10.720
from children can affect the
progression of the epidemic.

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:13.440
So we'll be speaking a lot about models.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:16.040
So let's have a look at
what this math model is

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:17.490
and how it's built.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.930
And something I wanna mention
before I go through it,

00:18:19.930 --> 00:18:22.470
is that the model that we present,

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:23.880
is the one used in the article.

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.510
But it's a classical model

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:27.630
that is used by a lot of researchers

00:18:29.705 --> 00:18:32.580
to model infectious disease
transmission in populations.

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:35.500
And so it would be the same
principle for other disease.

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:39.810
So that's basically the model.

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:41.450
And what we do is,

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:44.930
basically we divide the
population into different groups.

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:48.840
And the groups depend on the
different stages of the people.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:52.570
So the people in white
here are the susceptible,

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:55.640
which is basically all of us
last year regarding COVID-19.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:58.440
It means that we've never seen the disease

00:18:58.440 --> 00:18:59.493
and we can catch it.

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:04.530
Then we've got the people
in pink and in red.

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:07.200
And those people are
infected by the virus,

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:09.103
so the virus is in their body.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:13.560
But only the people in
red here are infectious,

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:15.360
which means that they can transmit

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.313
to the susceptible people in white.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:21.720
And the infected people who
cannot transmit the virus,

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:24.553
so the people in pink
here, are called exposed.

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:27.240
And finally, in blue,

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:30.920
are the people who recovered
from the infection,

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:33.913
or unfortunately, in some
cases, die from the infection.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:36.630
So now that we have our groups,

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:39.730
you may wonder what are
the arrows in the group

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:40.680
and what they mean.

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:44.360
So let's take a simple example.

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:47.390
So at the moment, it's
very likely that myself,

00:19:47.390 --> 00:19:50.480
am in a susceptible group, in white.

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:53.010
And so you can imagine
that tomorrow for example,

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:55.680
I go do laundry in the
basement of the building,

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.900
and I chat with my neighbors.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:00.860
But the issue is my neighbor is infectious

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:02.440
and he doesn't know it.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:05.510
And he also had a very bad
idea of not wearing a mask

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:08.470
when he go to do the laundry.

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:10.960
So by being in contact with him,

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:14.510
I catch the virus and I become infected,

00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:17.193
without being able to
transmit the virus right away.

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:20.810
So that means that I'm moving

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:24.600
from the susceptible group
to the exposed group.

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:27.433
And that process of moving
from one group to another,

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.160
from the susceptible to exposed,

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:32.203
is defined by the transmission rate,

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:36.010
which as Sinead explained,
is determining, on average,

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:37.620
how many people my neighbor

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:40.730
will transmit the virus each day.

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:43.380
And unfortunately, I'm part
of those people that day.

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:47.860
Then I stay in this exposed stage.

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:50.010
So I am infected by the virus,

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:52.060
but I cannot transmit it yet.

00:20:52.060 --> 00:20:56.250
And that amount of time is
defined by the latent period.

00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:58.750
So after that period,
which is gonna last around,

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:00.580
let's say, five days.

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:02.010
I'm gonna become infectious,

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:04.530
and then I can transmit the disease.

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:05.820
So when I am infectious,

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:08.490
I can transmit the virus to
other susceptible persons

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:10.190
if I don't stay home, for example.

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:16.270
And then finally, I'm
able to transmit the virus

00:21:16.270 --> 00:21:18.380
during the infectious period.

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:22.230
And after that time, which
lasts roughly seven days,

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:23.623
I'll hopefully recover.

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:28.920
So now you know that the
arrows represents how

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:31.523
and how fast people move
from one group to another one

00:21:31.523 --> 00:21:33.440
when in the model.

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:35.410
And basically what we do with the model,

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:39.740
is making sure that everyone
belongs to one of the group.

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:43.820
And every day we look at what
has changed in the population.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:46.060
So today, I'm in the susceptible group

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:48.640
and my neighbor is in
the infectious group.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:50.790
And tomorrow, the model which will predict

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:53.180
that I am in the exposed group,

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:55.060
and so on every day.

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:57.170
And so what we are interested in,

00:21:57.170 --> 00:22:00.310
is how many new cases
we will have every day,

00:22:00.310 --> 00:22:02.180
Which is basically how many people move

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:04.993
from the susceptible to
the exposed group everyday.

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:08.010
And then finally, there's something

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:10.180
that we haven't mentioned yet.

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:11.960
But you all probably know that,

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:13.720
not everyone develops symptoms

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:16.143
when they are infected by the virus.

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:19.730
And so we can divide the
group of infectious people

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:22.720
into two additional subgroups of people.

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:24.610
So we have the people with symptoms

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:26.730
and the people without symptoms.

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:30.210
And for example, based on
the first observations,

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:33.750
we think that children will
have less symptoms than adults

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:36.060
and so they will more
likely be in that group

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:37.760
than the other one, with symptoms.

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.063
- Okay, so Chloé just
went through the models

00:22:42.063 --> 00:22:43.300
that the authors use,

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:44.420
and now I'll go through

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:47.263
how they incorporate social
distancing into that model.

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:50.560
But first, going back to
the transmission rate,

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:53.160
we said that it depends
on the number of contacts

00:22:53.160 --> 00:22:54.530
that each person has,

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:56.370
and the chance that
each of those contacts,

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:58.423
actually results in a new infection.

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:02.040
There is something that we didn't mention,

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:04.010
is that the number of
contacts the person has,

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:06.030
can depend on their age.

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:07.120
So for example, if we think

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:08.850
about what happens during the day,

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:10.700
we might expect that working adults,

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:13.740
will mainly come into contact
with some other adults,

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:15.330
whereas children may come in contact

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:19.120
with lots of other children if
they go to school or nursery.

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:22.110
And the elderly may come into
contact with some other adults

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:24.020
and some other elderly people,

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:26.390
for example, if they live
with partners of the same age,

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:28.420
or if they live in a care home.

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:30.630
So these differences are
actually very important

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:33.010
when you start to think about the impact

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:35.010
of different social distancing measures.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:39.360
For example, if we think
about closing schools,

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:40.820
then that will mainly reduce the number

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:43.020
of contacts among children.

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:45.820
Whereas if we introduce social
distancing in the workplace,

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:48.020
so-called workplace distancing,

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:49.160
then that will reduce number

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:51.083
of contacts mainly among adults.

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:54.140
And then if we introduce social distancing

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:57.120
in the community or
general social distancing,

00:23:57.120 --> 00:23:59.330
such as closing public spaces,

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:02.010
or stopping people visiting
their friends and family,

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:05.040
then that will reduce the number
of contacts among everyone.

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:06.970
But here we've shown how it can impact contacts

00:24:06.970 --> 00:24:08.773
of the elderly just as an example.

00:24:10.031 --> 00:24:10.990
And the main point here,

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:13.130
is that different social
distancing measures

00:24:13.130 --> 00:24:16.140
will reduce the number of
contacts in different age groups.

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:16.980
And as a result,

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:20.040
will reduce the transmission
rate in those age groups.

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:22.910
And so incorporating social
distancing into the model,

00:24:22.910 --> 00:24:25.010
is the same as reducing
the transmission rate

00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:26.870
in each of these age groups.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:29.260
And as Chloé said, that's
just like reducing the number

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:31.620
of people who move from
that susceptible group

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:33.363
into those infected groups.

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:38.990
Okay, so how does this
all relate to the paper?

00:24:38.990 --> 00:24:40.010
Well, in the paper,

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:41.270
they explore what would happen

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:44.253
in four different social
distancing scenarios in Wuhan.

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:47.230
So the first scenario, in purple,

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:49.290
is if no social distancing was in place,

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:52.440
so essentially daily
life goes on as normal.

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:55.410
The second scenario, in blue,
is if schools were closed,

00:24:55.410 --> 00:24:57.710
and workplace distancing was introduced,

00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:00.293
but only for a month between
January and February.

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:02.580
The third scenario, in orange,

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:05.950
is if general social
distancing was also introduced,

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:08.410
though all three measures
are in place from January

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:10.410
and then this goes on till March.

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:12.060
And then scenario four, in yellow,

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:13.860
is the same as scenario three,

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:15.380
except instead of ending in March,

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:17.243
it runs all the way through to April.

00:25:18.384 --> 00:25:19.620
And so there are a lot of details

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:21.040
about these different scenarios,

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:23.020
but you don't really need
to remember them all.

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:24.740
The main thing to remember is that,

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:27.147
scenario one is when there's
no social distancing.

00:25:27.147 --> 00:25:29.880
And then as you move up from one to four,

00:25:29.880 --> 00:25:31.560
the measures get more intense

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:34.530
and are in place for
longer periods of time.

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:36.890
But once each of these rules are lifted,

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:38.273
life goes back to normal.

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:40.880
And so for objective one,

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:42.160
the authors wanted to explore

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:43.720
what would happen in Wuhan

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:45.853
when in each of these different scenarios.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:50.050
And so before we show you what they found,

00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:53.160
I just want to take you
through the axes of this figure

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:54.960
'cause there's quite a lot going on.

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:58.200
So along the x-axis we have the time

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:00.640
since the virus first emerged in Wuhan

00:26:00.640 --> 00:26:02.270
and it starts in November last year

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:04.740
and it runs all the way through
to December of this year,

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:06.610
because the authors wanted
to look at what could happen

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:08.320
in the coming months.

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:09.580
And then along the y-axis,

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:12.730
we have the number of new
cases per day, in thousands.

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:13.690
And so for example,

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:16.740
a value of 60 would mean there
were 60,000 new infections

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:17.573
on that day.

00:26:18.730 --> 00:26:19.563
And then finally,

00:26:19.563 --> 00:26:21.610
the colored vertical
bars represent the times

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:23.460
when those different
social distancing scenarios

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:24.910
were in place.

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:26.560
So there's no purple bar

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:28.940
because there was no
social distancing in place

00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:30.300
in the first scenario.

00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:32.890
And then as you move from
blue, to orange, to yellow,

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:34.830
you can see that the
scenarios were in place

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:37.213
till either February, March or April.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.570
And so now we've got our bearings,

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:44.530
we can look at what the
model simulations look like.

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:46.750
And again, there is quite a
lot going on in this figure

00:26:46.750 --> 00:26:50.010
so if we first just focus
on those first two curves,

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:50.930
scenario one, in purple,

00:26:50.930 --> 00:26:53.420
was when there is no social distancing

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:56.790
and then two was when schools were closed

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:58.740
and there was workplace distancing,

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:00.223
but only until February.

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:02.650
And you can see that by adding in

00:27:02.650 --> 00:27:04.630
just a little bit social distancing,

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:06.520
we managed to reduce the number of cases

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:08.100
at the peak of the outbreak.

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:08.933
And that's that day

00:27:08.933 --> 00:27:11.210
when you have the highest number of cases.

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:14.550
And we also managed to
delay the peak a little bit.

00:27:14.550 --> 00:27:15.743
And so essentially, that's what we mean

00:27:15.743 --> 00:27:17.870
when we say flatten the curve,

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:19.460
that we managed to reduce

00:27:19.460 --> 00:27:21.393
and delay the peak of the outbreak.

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:25.070
Now, if we focus on the last two curves,

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:27.850
scenario three was when
general social distancing

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:31.210
was also added, and this
was in place till march.

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:32.660
And then scenario four was the same,

00:27:32.660 --> 00:27:34.360
except it was in place till April.

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:38.000
And now you can see we
make even more progress

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:40.327
towards delaying and reducing that peak.

00:27:40.327 --> 00:27:43.510
And so these strict social
distancing measures,

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:45.693
improve our ability to flatten the curve.

00:27:47.120 --> 00:27:48.280
As I'm sure you know that,

00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:50.620
this is important because
it gives governments

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:52.900
and health systems more time to prepare.

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:54.980
And it means that there
will be fewer patients

00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:57.770
for them to treat when the
outbreak's at its peak,

00:27:57.770 --> 00:27:58.820
which means there's less chance

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:00.763
of hospital systems being overwhelmed.

00:28:04.010 --> 00:28:06.450
- Okay, so now let's look
at the second objective

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:07.320
of the paper.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:10.570
And let me walk you
through this slide also.

00:28:10.570 --> 00:28:13.280
So here the question is how transmission

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:15.790
from children affects the outbreak?

00:28:15.790 --> 00:28:17.150
And now what we're gonna do is,

00:28:17.150 --> 00:28:19.240
just focus on the best case scenario,

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:21.260
which is the fourth scenario in yellow,

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:23.060
that Sinead just presented,

00:28:23.060 --> 00:28:25.300
that was able to delay
the epidemic the most

00:28:25.300 --> 00:28:28.970
and substantially reduce the
number of cases at the peak.

00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:30.500
And so in order to measure

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:32.500
and quantify the impact

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:35.490
of taking this strict
social distancing measures

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:37.020
on the outbreak,

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:40.280
the authors look at how
many cases can be avoided

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:42.320
until the end of 2020

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:45.440
by applying this strict
social distancing rules,

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:47.150
compared to the worst case scenario

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:48.920
where nothing would have been done,

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:51.020
which is the one in purple, the first one.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:54.000
And then since we have uncertainties

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:56.530
on how infectious children are,

00:28:56.530 --> 00:29:00.390
in each graph, the authors
have run a different simulation

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:01.670
to explore what happens

00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:03.920
when children are as infectious as adults,

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:05.900
and that's the graph on the left,

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:09.010
or what happens when
they are less infectious,

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:10.777
which is the graph on the right.

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:16.690
So now, let's just focus
on the graph on the left.

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:20.530
So children are assumed to
be as infectious as adults.

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:22.840
And the y-axis here,

00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:25.790
is the percentage of cases avoided

00:29:25.790 --> 00:29:28.540
by using the most intense
social distancing measures,

00:29:28.540 --> 00:29:30.670
compared to doing nothing.

00:29:30.670 --> 00:29:33.320
And the x-axis is breaking this down

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:35.120
among different age groups in Wuhan.

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:37.920
So you can see that the biggest percentage

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:41.000
of cases which is here and here,

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:43.620
is around 50% or more,

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:45.600
and that was avoided in young people

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:48.340
and also the elderly, so here.

00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:51.080
And this is particularly
important for the elderly

00:29:51.080 --> 00:29:53.710
because they are the ones
who are the most at risk

00:29:53.710 --> 00:29:55.260
of severe disease.

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:56.880
So what this graph show is that,

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:59.830
by introducing strict
social distancing methods,

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:01.610
around half of the case,

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:03.890
we could have expected by doing nothing

00:30:03.890 --> 00:30:06.700
or avoided in this vulnerable population

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:09.100
by doing these strict
social distancing rules,

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:10.533
and that's really great.

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:15.100
And then if you look at
the graph on the right,

00:30:15.100 --> 00:30:18.840
the authors assume children are
less infectious than adults,

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:23.110
and you can see that this
certain cases of avoided cases,

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:27.150
jumps from around 50
persons to 80 persons.

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:28.610
And that's a big difference.

00:30:28.610 --> 00:30:30.540
And that shows that it's really important

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:32.550
to understand how much children contribute

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:34.133
to the spread of the disease.

00:30:38.050 --> 00:30:40.230
- Okay, so now that we've
gone over the main findings

00:30:40.230 --> 00:30:42.820
from the paper, we can go
back to these two objectives

00:30:42.820 --> 00:30:45.380
and see how the authors answered them.

00:30:45.380 --> 00:30:46.640
So for the first objective,

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:48.490
they wanted to use a model to predict

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:50.070
what would happen in Wuhan

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:52.513
if and when social
distancing rules were lifted.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:55.203
And what they found from the first figure

00:30:55.203 --> 00:30:57.620
that we discussed is
that social distancing

00:30:57.620 --> 00:30:58.963
helped flatten the curve.

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:01.920
And so at this point
you could rightly tell

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:04.550
is that you would have predicted
this from the beginning.

00:31:04.550 --> 00:31:05.790
But where the models really help,

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:08.490
is by putting numbers behind this idea.

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:11.482
So it's important to know, for example,

00:31:11.482 --> 00:31:13.770
how many cases we might expect each day.

00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:16.300
And by how much time we
might delay the peak,

00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:19.760
so that hospitals and
governments can plan accordingly.

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:21.170
And where the model also helps us,

00:31:21.170 --> 00:31:23.120
is to compare different scenarios

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:25.870
and to put a number on
how much we might gain

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:29.093
if we use those stricter
social distancing measures.

00:31:31.979 --> 00:31:33.620
- In the second objective,

00:31:33.620 --> 00:31:35.810
they aim at exploring how transmission

00:31:35.810 --> 00:31:38.580
from children affect the outbreak.

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:41.150
And what they found from that
second graph I showed you,

00:31:41.150 --> 00:31:44.350
is that if children are
less infectious than adults,

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:46.070
we can avoid even more cases

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:48.150
with those social distancing rules.

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:50.170
And again, the model helps putting numbers

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:51.513
behind this concept.

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:54.420
And there's still uncertainty actually

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:56.440
about how children are
affected by the disease

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:57.900
and so this study highlight

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:00.920
that we need to do more
research about that.

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:02.310
So one thing to keep in mind,

00:32:02.310 --> 00:32:05.060
is that the study was made in Wuhan.

00:32:05.060 --> 00:32:07.250
And the social mixing in Wuhan,

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:09.020
may be different from other cities

00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:11.010
like New York, for example.

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:13.080
And so the results from
the same type of model

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:14.753
in New York will be different.

00:32:15.730 --> 00:32:19.290
Another point is that this
study was conducted in March,

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:22.520
when social distancing rules
were still in place in Wuhan.

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:24.940
And now we know that those
rules have been lifted

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:27.270
and so the situation in
new Wuhan has changed.

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:30.050
And if the authors want to
incorporate these changes,

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:31.940
they will need to gather more data

00:32:31.940 --> 00:32:32.900
and update the model

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:36.210
to reflect the more recent circumstances.

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:39.020
So keeping those two points in mind,

00:32:39.020 --> 00:32:40.410
I just wanna quickly show you

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:43.940
how the model can be
applied in other contexts.

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:45.970
And here what you can
see is some researchers

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:48.580
from Columbia University who
are projecting the number

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:50.850
of new infections in New York City,

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:53.680
and they actually regularly
update the predictions

00:32:53.680 --> 00:32:55.680
using the collected data.

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:56.940
So here for example,

00:32:56.940 --> 00:32:59.063
I took what they did on May 3rd.

00:33:00.110 --> 00:33:03.600
So they projected what
would happen eight weeks

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:08.320
after May 3rd, by using data
that was collected before.

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:10.340
So the blue part correspond to the data

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:12.840
and it's actually the
same in the two graphics.

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:15.520
What changes is the scale of the y-axis.

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:18.470
So here you see this 30,000 is here,

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:21.145
while in this graph, it's here.

00:33:21.145 --> 00:33:23.673
A different scale, but it's
actually the same data,

00:33:23.673 --> 00:33:26.320
it's the same graph, the one in blue.

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:27.490
And then the yellow part,

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:31.370
which is on the right of
this dash line here and here,

00:33:31.370 --> 00:33:35.570
it correspond to the
projection under the worst case

00:33:35.570 --> 00:33:38.150
on the left, meaning that
all of the restrictions

00:33:38.150 --> 00:33:40.410
are lifted in New York

00:33:40.410 --> 00:33:44.690
and if we keep the restrictions
as they are at the moment,

00:33:44.690 --> 00:33:46.380
so on the right.

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:48.710
And then these two yellow lines here,

00:33:48.710 --> 00:33:50.600
in dash and in plain line,

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:52.750
they just correspond to
different simulations

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:54.183
that the authors made,

00:33:54.183 --> 00:33:56.630
that are based on different hypothesis

00:33:56.630 --> 00:33:58.303
on the aspects of the
transmission of the virus

00:33:58.303 --> 00:34:00.060
that we don't fully know.

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:01.710
So they just took different branches

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:03.310
as I explained at the beginning.

00:34:05.275 --> 00:34:06.260
And then something else

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:08.150
that we could do with this type of model,

00:34:08.150 --> 00:34:10.560
is studying other type of interventions

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:13.210
in addition to the
social distancing rules.

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:15.860
So for example, if a
vaccine become available,

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:19.500
we can look at how the
progression of the outbreak

00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:22.380
is modified by introducing
the vaccine in the population,

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:24.840
using the same type of model.

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:26.670
So basically, what we did at the beginning

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:29.910
of the epidemic, is looking
at how the disease was spread

00:34:29.910 --> 00:34:32.700
in a population that is
completely susceptible.

00:34:32.700 --> 00:34:36.930
And that's that type of
population here on the left.

00:34:36.930 --> 00:34:38.880
And now what we're looking at,

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:40.850
is what happens when the transmission

00:34:40.850 --> 00:34:43.450
is in a more heterogeneous population.

00:34:43.450 --> 00:34:45.010
So here what's happening now,

00:34:45.010 --> 00:34:47.170
where we have people who
are still infectious,

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:48.530
people who've recovered,

00:34:48.530 --> 00:34:50.480
people who were also still susceptible.

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:54.210
We can also look at how the progression

00:34:54.210 --> 00:34:56.160
of the disease will be modified

00:34:56.160 --> 00:34:57.740
and hopefully controlled,

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:00.540
by vaccinating a certain
amount of the population,

00:35:00.540 --> 00:35:02.190
here in yellow.

00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:04.880
And we assume that those
people who are vaccinated,

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:07.330
they're not at risk of
catching the disease

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:09.103
if the vaccine is efficient enough.

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:15.270
And then something, finally
that Sinead and I wanted to highlight,

00:35:15.270 --> 00:35:18.840
is that the models depend
on the accuracy of the data.

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:20.610
So you may have heard
that at the beginning,

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:21.740
the models were not good.

00:35:21.740 --> 00:35:24.000
But there was not enough data

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:25.620
to make really great predictions.

00:35:25.620 --> 00:35:27.810
And we saw we can refine the estimation

00:35:27.810 --> 00:35:29.410
and the predictions that
we do with the model

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:31.683
by gathering more data on the epidemic.

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:34.933
So on that note,

00:35:34.933 --> 00:35:38.070
we just wanted to provide
some additional resources,

00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:41.030
where you can look at up-to-date models

00:35:41.030 --> 00:35:44.530
and see how they're using real
time outbreak forecasting.

00:35:44.530 --> 00:35:46.240
So for example, the first tool here,

00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:49.840
is an interactive tool that
is created by the authors

00:35:49.840 --> 00:35:52.740
and also other scientists
they collaborate with.

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:54.920
And you can actually
modify some parameters

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:56.490
and explore by yourself the impact

00:35:56.490 --> 00:36:00.190
of different social distancing
measures on the outbreak

00:36:00.190 --> 00:36:01.340
in different countries.

00:36:02.220 --> 00:36:03.660
And the second link,

00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:05.460
it's actually the CDC website

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:07.160
where you will have results

00:36:07.160 --> 00:36:10.380
and predictions from different
models, from different teams.

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:12.350
And something we wanna conclude with,

00:36:12.350 --> 00:36:14.780
is that it's really important
to look at the results

00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:17.350
from different models based
on different assumptions,

00:36:17.350 --> 00:36:19.890
different data, because
public health decisions

00:36:19.890 --> 00:36:22.910
are not taken only based on
the results of one model,

00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:25.010
but they're more based on
the combination of results

00:36:25.010 --> 00:36:27.260
from different studies
with different models.

